```

</details>

Also I've been using the new estimator to see how it behaves. I was worried that the mempool had a low respresnatation ratio or that it was too sparse to fill 95% of a block. After collecting data for 3 days, I've seen this concerns were not justified.

<details>
<summary>You can see here the results of the data I gathered:</summary>

What I did was to call the `estimatesmartfee` rpc every 7 seconds, with the parameters `conf_target=6`, `estimate_mode=economical` and `{block_policy_only: false, verbosity: 2}`. I ran my own version so the rpc returns the reason of why the mempool estimator was not choosen. Between calls I also used `getmempoolinfo` to get general information about the state of the mempool.

This are the results I got:

<img width="2100" height="3000" alt="fee_monitor_charts_2" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5ad304b0-6ec0-4ac1-8d23-3a9222dce9e6" />

_In the graph, the backround blue represents that the mempool estimator was choosend, and the backround amber reresents that block policy was choosen._

We have that more or less 60% of the times the block policy estimator is used, and the other 40% the mempool estimator. The representation ratio, is always higher than 90%, so unless a user have too many mempool policies, it shouldn't be difficult to have it over the 75% threshold (I was worried that it were difficult to get to this thershold).

All the time that the block estimator was choosend over the mempool one, it was because the block estimator returned a lower feerate, so at least for the past 3 days, the mempool estimator did not have any problems regarding the `ismempoolhealthy`.

I've noticed that when the estimator is set to the block policy, the returned fee rate exhibits spikes, which may lead to an overestimation. In contrast, when the mempool estimator is selected, the estimated fee rate is generally more stable. Also, the block policy estimator tends to be selected when fee rates in the mempool vary significantly, whereas the mempool estimator produces more consistent fee estimates even under those conditions.

</details>

